# Build context is the repo root (deploy/docker/service.Dockerfile).
# Keep the context tiny: only the Python workspace enters the images.

# VCS / agent org / docs
.git
.github
.claude
docs
*.md

# Environments & caches
.venv
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache
.env
.env.*

# Node / frontend. Excluded here and RE-INCLUDED at the bottom of this file for
# the web image (deploy/docker/web.Dockerfile) — the ordering matters, so the
# negations live together in one block down there rather than being inverted
# here.
node_modules
apps
packages/design
packages/ts-client
packages/contracts
pnpm-lock.yaml
pnpm-workspace.yaml
package.json
eslint.config.js

# Tests never ship in runtime images
**/tests

# Deploy assets themselves (Dockerfile is passed via -f)
deploy
docker-compose*.yml
justfile
scripts

# …except the licence machinery, which MUST enter the image. A container image
# redistributes every byte in it (docs/LICENSING.md §1): the licence texts are
# an LGPL-2.1 §6 obligation, strip-gpl-jbig.sh removes the GPL-2.0 library the
# OCP wheel vendors, and check-licences.py is the build-time gate that proves
# both. Excluding these silently is exactly how the obligation gets dropped.
#
# THIS LIST IS LOAD-BEARING AND IT HAS LOST AN ENTRY BEFORE. A COPY whose source
# is excluded here resolves to nothing and fails the build — but only at
# `docker build` time, which the blocked registry puts out of local reach, so it
# lands in CI's `deploy-path` and nowhere earlier. That is exactly how LIC-2's
# `scripts/corresponding_source.py` (imported by check-licences.py) broke all
# three images for two commits on 2026-08-01. `scripts/check-build-context.py`
# now re-implements Docker's matching and asserts every COPY source survives
# these rules; it runs in `just lint` and CI's `python` job, so the next omission
# fails in seconds instead of twenty minutes.
!deploy/docker/licence
!deploy/licenses
!scripts/check-licences.py
!scripts/corresponding_source.py

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE WEB IMAGE'S CONTEXT (deploy/docker/web.Dockerfile).
#
# One .dockerignore serves every build in this context, so these negations also
# widen the three SERVICE image contexts by ~8 MB of TypeScript they do not
# COPY. That is the price of Docker's one-ignore-file-per-context rule; the
# alternative (a sibling `web.Dockerfile.dockerignore`, which BuildKit does
# honour) would be invisible to scripts/check-build-context.py and would fall
# back to THIS file under the classic builder — i.e. it would fail in exactly
# the place we cannot test. Context transfer is local I/O; correctness that can
# be gated statically is worth more.
#
# The SPA build needs the workspace root manifests, apps/web, and the two
# source-only packages it imports (@loft/design, @loft/ts-client).
# packages/contracts is here because pnpm-workspace.yaml's `packages/*` glob
# makes it a workspace member: with it absent, `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`
# would be comparing a different workspace against the lockfile.
!package.json
!pnpm-lock.yaml
!pnpm-workspace.yaml
!apps/web
!packages/design
!packages/ts-client
!packages/contracts
!deploy/docker/web

# …and then re-exclude the heavy or useless subtrees the negations above would
# otherwise drag in. These MUST come last: `.dockerignore` is last-match-wins,
# so the same rules written above the negations would simply be overridden.
# `node_modules` near the top of this file matches only the ROOT one (a pattern
# with no slash is anchored, exactly as in Docker), which is why the `**/`
# form is repeated here — `apps/web/node_modules` alone is hundreds of MB and
# would be the wrong platform's binaries besides.
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/test-results
**/playwright-report
**/tests
apps/web/e2e
